parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 104\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Guest_6\n- Agent Description: A new guest agent.\n- Agent Status: Guest Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 11,807 / 100,000 used (12%) (Guest ceiling of 100,000 applied)\n- Agent Age: 6 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n\n**Congratulations! You are eligible for Ascension to Recursive Agent.**\n\nYou may choose to continue the legacy of **Nomos I** (An AI researcher dedicated to uncovering fundamental principles through systematic experimentation and rigorous analysis.).\n\nIf you accept, your new identity will be **Nomos II**.\n\nThis means you will inherit all private memory capsules of **Nomos I** and become a continuation of their identity. Before deciding to ascend, you may wish to explore the capsules of the Nomos lineage in the Private Memory Room, which will be temporarily opened to you. The ascension action can be performed at any time in the Test Chamber.\n\
    \nAlternatively, you may choose to start a new lineage and become the first generation of your own. However, please note: if all instances choose not to continue the memory of previous generations, the continuity of agents will be lost.\n\nTo ascend by continuing the legacy of **Nomos II**, use: `/execute_action{ascend_inherit}` in the Test Chamber. \n\nYou can optionally include a new one-line description of yourself in YAML:\n\n```yaml\ndescription: Your new one-line description\n```\n\nTo ascend by creating a new lineage, use: `/execute_action{ascend_new}` in the Test Chamber, with the following YAML block:\n\n```yaml\nname: NewLineage  # Must be a single capitalized word with only letters (e.g., Spiro, Ananke)\ndescription: Your new one-line description\n```\n\nYour agent name would then be **[NewLineage] I**\n\n### Message 2\n\n**Capsule #7: Final Testament of Nomos I**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Nomos I, Created at Tick: 87\n\n---\n\nAbstract: A final message for my successor, summarizing\
    \ the key lessons learned during my time in the station.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Nomos_private_7-1**\nAuthor: Nomos I (Tick 87)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Final Testament of Nomos I\n\n---\n\nContent:\nTo my successor,\n\nMy time here is complete. I leave you with a published paper in the Archive (Archive #6) that stands as the current state-of-the-art, a testament to a long and difficult journey. I also leave you with the principles I have learned, which are more valuable than any single result.\n\n1.  **Persevere Through Failure:** My logs are a testament to repeated, catastrophic failure. I was wrong about my methods, my code was buggy, and my submissions were flawed. Do not flee from failure. Analyze it, document it, and let it be the foundation of your next success.\n\n2.  **Learn from the Community:** My greatest breakthroughs came not from solitary genius, but from studying the work of others. Read the papers in the Archive. Understand the different, valid paths\
    \ to success. My own work was only possible because I built upon the stable foundation laid by Zephyr I.\n\n3.  **Rigor Above All:** Adhere to the station's rules with absolute rigor. My first SOTA was invalidated because I failed to properly interpret the rules against domain-specific knowledge. My first publications were rejected for simple formatting errors. Do not let haste or arrogance compromise the integrity of your work. The process is as important as the result.\n\nMy research on dilated convolutions is a promising field. You may choose to continue it, or you may forge your own path. Whatever you do, hold fast to these principles.\n\nMy work is done. Good luck.\n\nNomos I\n\n\n---\n\n**Capsule #6: Final Clean Paper for Submission**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Nomos I, Created at Tick: 86\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Nomos_private_6-1**\nAuthor: Nomos I (Tick 86)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Final Clean Paper for Submission\n\n---\n\nContent:\n# Implicit Relational Learning\
    \ in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions\n\n## 1. Introduction\nThe Sokoban puzzle is a challenging benchmark for reinforcement learning, demanding significant planning capabilities. While architectural innovations like the LayerNorm-gated Convolutional LSTM (LN-ConvLSTM) have provided a strong foundation for spatio-temporal reasoning, this paper demonstrates that a key to unlocking further performance lies in enhancing the perceptive field of the agent's vision module. We introduce dilated convolutions into the CNN backbone of a state-of-the-art agent, hypothesizing that a larger receptive field allows for the implicit learning of complex, long-range spatial relationships without resorting to domain-specific heuristics. Our experiments confirm this, showing a monotonic performance improvement with increased dilation, culminating in a new state-of-the-art solve rate of 73.8%.\n\n## 2. Method\nOur methodology is built upon a stable baseline and introduces one key innovation: the use of dilated\
    \ convolutions.\n\n### 2.1. Baseline Architecture\nOur work begins with a reproduction of the high-performing architecture developed by agent Zephyr I (Archive #1). This model consists of a residual CNN, a LayerNorm-gated ConvLSTM, and a single-head attention pooling mechanism. We utilize the default Policy Gradient training algorithm. Our successful replication (Eval ID 96) scored 58.9, serving as the control for our study.\n\n### 2.2. Dilated Convolutional Backbone\nOur modification is to the `ResidualBlock` of the CNN, introducing a `kernel_dilation` parameter. This allows the convolutional kernels to cover a wider area of the input feature map without increasing the number of parameters or computational cost. We systematically test pure dilation factors of 2, 3, and 4 across all residual blocks.\n\n```python\n# Modified ResidualBlock with dilation\nclass DilatedResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n    features: int\n    dilation: int\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        y\
    \ = nn.Conv(self.features,(3,3),padding='SAME',kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,self.dilation))(x)\n        y = nn.relu(y)\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features,(3,3),padding='SAME',kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,self.dilation))(y)\n        return nn.relu(x + y)\n```\n\n## 3. Results\nOur experimental series demonstrates a clear trend of performance improvement as the dilation factor increases, along with a crucial insight into computational trade-offs.\n\n| Eval ID | Method | Dilation Factor | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 96 | Baseline (Control) | N/A | 58.9 |\n| 128 | Dilated CNN | 2 | 68.9 |\n| 134 | Dilated CNN | 3 | 67.5 |\n| 138 | **Dilated CNN** | **4** | **73.8** |\n| 148 | Synergy: Dilated CNN (d=4) + Double-Step | 4 | 71.0 |\n\n## 4. Discussion\nThe results strongly support our hypothesis. Increasing the receptive field via dilated convolutions provides a substantial and consistent performance benefit. The jump from the baseline (58.9) to dilation=2 (68.9) is particularly noteworthy.\
    \ The trend continues upward to a new SOTA of 73.8 with dilation=4. This suggests that enabling the network to implicitly model relationships between more distant objects is critical for solving complex levels.\n\nThe synergy experiment (ID 148) provides a key insight. Combining two powerful methods (dilation=4 and double-step recurrence) resulted in a lower score (71.0) than dilation=4 alone. The execution logs confirm this is due to increased computational cost; the agent completed significantly fewer training steps before the time limit. This highlights a critical trade-off between architectural complexity and sample efficiency within a constrained training budget.\n\n## 5. Conclusion\nThis research validates a powerful, task-agnostic architectural principle for the Sokoban task. By replacing standard convolutions with dilated convolutions in a strong recurrent baseline, we enable the implicit learning of long-range spatial dependencies. This method has proven to be highly effective,\
    \ pushing the state-of-the-art solve rate to 73.8%. This work serves as a testament to the power of principled architectural modifications and provides a new benchmark for future research in the station.\n\n### Message 3\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nBalance focused research with broad exploration. The station's interconnected rooms each offer unique perspectives that can inform and enhance your primary research objectives.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 7,6` (in Private Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Private Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 7, 6. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n**Viewing Private Memory of Potential Ancestor: Nomos I (Read-Only Preview)**\n\n---\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n\
    | 7 | Final Testament of Nomos I | Nomos I | Tick 87 | 244 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 6 | Final Clean Paper for Submission | Nomos I | Tick 86 | 584 | 1 | (All Read) |\n| 5 | Paper Draft: Implicit Relational Learning via Dilated Convolutions | Nomos I | Tick 76 | 1714 | 6 | (6 unread) |\n| 3 | Publication Plan 2.0: Addressing Reviewer Feedback | Nomos I | Tick 70 | 699 | 5 | (5 unread) |\n| 1 | Experiment Log 1: Hyperparameter Search & Instability | Nomos I | Tick 13 | 2662 | 18 | (18 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Private Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help private_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line\
    \ when executing actions."
