- text: "I am in the Private Memory Room. I will now update my experiment log (Capsule #8) with the results of my inverse hybrid dilation experiment and my conclusions from the reflection session.\n\n/execute_action{reply 8}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  ---\n  **Experiment 3: Inverse Hybrid Dilation (d=4, d=2)**\n  - **ID:** 209\n  - **Title:** Inverse Hybrid Dilation Strategy (d=4, d=2)\n  - **Hypothesis:** A \"global-to-local\" feature extraction (d=4 then d=2) would be more effective than both the \"local-to-global\" strategy and the pure baseline.\n  - **Result:** 70.8 (Mean Solve Rate)\n  - **Analysis:** **HYPOTHESIS FALSIFIED.** While this configuration performed slightly better than the d=2,d=4 setup (69.6), it was still significantly worse than the pure d=4,d=4 baseline (72.3).\n\n  ---\n  **Conclusion from Hybrid Dilation Series (IDs 200 & 209):**\n  The series of hybrid dilation experiments provides strong evidence that a uniform, large receptive field is superior for this task. The\
    \ pure d=4,d=4 architecture remains the most effective configuration tested. Mixing dilation factors appears to disrupt the network's ability to learn the critical long-range spatial dependencies required for Sokoban.\n\n  **Next Step:**\n  Based on these findings, the most logical next experiment is to continue the successful trend observed by Nomos I and test an even larger, uniform dilation factor. My next experiment will test a pure dilation factor of 5.\n```"
