parts:
- text: "I am in the Private Memory Room. I will now update my log with the results from the dilation=5 experiment and my analysis of Nomos I's failed capacity experiment.\n\n/execute_action{reply 8}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  ---\n  **Experiment 4: Testing Dilation Trend (d=5)**\n  - **ID:** 220\n  - **Title:** Testing Dilation Trend: Pure Dilation=5\n  - **Hypothesis:** The trend of performance improvement would continue with a pure dilation factor of 5.\n  - **Result:** 73.1 (Mean Solve Rate)\n  - **Analysis:** **HYPOTHESIS FALSIFIED.** The performance did not surpass the SOTA of 73.8 (d=4). The trend of monotonic improvement has ended. This provides strong evidence that a dilation factor of 4 is optimal for this architecture and that further increases to the receptive field are no longer beneficial. This line of inquiry is now considered complete.\n\n  ---\n  **Analysis of Nomos I's Failed Experiment (ID 169):**\n  - **Title:** Synergy Test: Dilated CNN (d=4) + Increased CNN Capacity\n\
    \  - **Original Score:** 47.7\n  - **My Analysis:** The execution logs reveal the cause of failure was not a flaw in the scientific premise, but a logistical one. The network, with doubled feature capacity (64, 128), was too computationally expensive. It completed only ~14,800 training updates before timing out, compared to the ~35,000 updates of the SOTA. The agent simply did not have enough time to learn.\n\n  ---\n  **New Research Direction: Network Capacity (Width)**\n  - **Conclusion:** The question of whether a wider network can improve performance remains unanswered. The failure of ID 169 was one of degree.\n  - **New Hypothesis:** A more modest increase in network width will provide a performance benefit by increasing representational capacity, without reducing the number of training updates so severely as to cripple learning.\n  - **Next Step:** Design an experiment that increases CNN features by a smaller amount, for example, from (32, 64) to (48, 96), a 50% increase. This\
    \ should provide a better balance between model capacity and training time.\n```"
