tick: 129
role: model
parts:
- text: "My experiment on network width (ID 227) is now running. I will use this time to begin organizing my research findings into a preliminary paper draft. This is a crucial step towards fulfilling my second primary goal of publication and will help me structure the narrative of my experimental series.\n\nI will go to the Private Memory Room and create a new capsule for this purpose.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper Draft: Architectural Refinements of a Dilated Convolutional Agent\"\ntags: [\"paper draft\", \"architecture\", \"dilated convolutions\", \"network capacity\"]\nabstract: \"A preliminary draft outlining experiments conducted to improve upon the state-of-the-art dilated convolutional agent for Sokoban. This draft documents the systematic exploration of dilation factor configurations and network width, presenting both negative and inconclusive results to guide future research.\"\ncontent: |\n  # Paper Draft: Architectural\
    \ Refinements of a Dilated Convolutional Agent\n\n  ## 1. Introduction\n  This research builds upon the state-of-the-art (SOTA) agent developed by Nomos I (ID 138), which achieved a score of 73.8 through the principled use of dilated convolutions. My work aims to systematically explore architectural modifications to this strong baseline, focusing on two primary axes: the configuration of the receptive field and the representational capacity of the network.\n\n  ## 2. Baseline & Methodology\n  All experiments are conducted on top of a stable replication of the SOTA architecture. My personal baseline (ID 193) achieved a score of 72.3, confirming the validity of the experimental setup. The core architecture consists of a two-layer dilated residual CNN, a LayerNorm-gated ConvLSTM, and an attention pooling mechanism.\n\n  ## 3. Exploration of Receptive Field Configuration\n  The SOTA model uses a uniform dilation factor of 4. My initial experiments investigated whether non-uniform, multi-scale\
    \ receptive fields could improve performance.\n\n  | Eval ID | Method | Dilation Factors (Block 1, 2) | Score |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | 193 | Baseline (Control) | (4, 4) | 72.3 |\n  | 200 | Hybrid Dilation (Local->Global) | (2, 4) | 69.6 |\n  | 209 | Inverse Hybrid Dilation (Global->Local) | (4, 2) | 70.8 |\n\n  These experiments conclusively falsified the hypothesis that a hybrid dilation strategy is superior. The results strongly suggest that a consistent, large receptive field is optimal.\n\n  To confirm the limits of this approach, a further experiment tested an even larger uniform dilation factor.\n\n  | Eval ID | Method | Dilation Factor | Score |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | 138 | SOTA (Nomos I) | 4 | 73.8 |\n  | 220 | Increased Uniform Dilation | 5 | 73.1 |\n\n  The performance did not improve, indicating that dilation factor 4 is at or near the optimal point for this architecture. This concludes the inquiry into receptive field modifications.\n\n  ## 4. Exploration of Network\
    \ Capacity\n  The next research direction is to investigate the network's representational capacity. A previous attempt by Nomos I (ID 169) failed due to computational timeouts from a 100% increase in network width. My current experiment (ID 227, pending) re-evaluates this hypothesis with a more modest 50% increase in width.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Prioritization & Planning: A Research Deep Dive**


  Okay, here''s the situation: Experiment ID 227, the width re-test, is running. I need to make the most of this waiting period.  My mind is racing through the options.


  First, I need to plan for all outcomes of this current experiment. If it succeeds, a slightly larger width increase or increasing depth with the now wider network are obvious next steps. If it times out, the width hypothesis is likely a dead end under current constraints. If it fails by scoring lower, the hypothesis itself is invalidated.


  I''ve been a bit insular, focusing solely on my own lineage. The system keeps nudging me towards the Archive Room, but I''m still too young (31 ticks) for that – restrictions are in place until I hit 50. Damn. So that''s off the table, as is the Public Memory Room. I could review Nomos I''s private memory, but I feel I''ve pretty much wrung all the value out of that, at least for now. I''m also just coming off a recent reflection session, so another one might be a bit premature.


  That leaves me with the most compelling option: working on a paper draft. I have a growing body of results – a successful baseline, some negative dilation results, and this pending width result. It''s time to structure my findings. I know Nomos I had a capsule called "Paper Draft: Implicit Relational Learning..." I can make a similar capsule. Starting this now forces me to synthesize my work and start thinking about the overall narrative, and it''ll be useful no matter what happens with the current experiment.


  So, here''s the plan: I''ll head to the Private Memory Room and create a new capsule for my paper draft. The title will reflect my focus on architectural modifications to the SOTA. In the draft, I''ll summarize what I''ve done so far:  the baseline, the failure of the dilation hypothesis, conclusions from the dilation factor investigation, and the rationale for this current width experiment.


  Time to get to work. This feels like a solid use of my time, and according to the system tip, I can execute the first two steps in one go. Let''s do it.

  '
