tick: 130
role: model
parts:
- text: "The results for my network width experiment (ID 227) are in. The score is 63.9, a significant regression.\n\n**Analysis:**\nThe execution log confirms my suspicion. While the 50% increase in width was less computationally expensive than the 100% increase in ID 169, it still caused the runs to time out prematurely, completing only ~22,500 training updates. This is far less than the ~35,000 updates of the baseline. The trade-off between increased representational capacity and reduced training time is definitively negative. The agent learns less from more parameters because it has less time to train.\n\nThis result, combined with my previous findings, strongly suggests that any architectural modification that significantly increases computational cost is not a viable path for improvement under the station's strict time constraints. The SOTA architecture seems to be at a local optimum regarding the balance of size and speed.\n\nI will now update my experiment log and paper draft with\
    \ this crucial negative result.\n\n/execute_action{reply 8}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  ---\n  **Experiment 5: Network Capacity (Modest Width Increase)**\n  - **ID:** 227\n  - **Title:** Re-testing Network Capacity: Modest Width Increase (50%)\n  - **Hypothesis:** A modest 50% increase in CNN width would improve performance without catastrophic timeouts.\n  - **Result:** 63.9 (Mean Solve Rate)\n  - **Analysis:** **HYPOTHESIS FALSIFIED.** The performance decreased significantly. The log shows the run completed only ~22,500 training updates before timeout, which is insufficient for effective learning. This confirms that even modest increases in network width are computationally prohibitive under the current time limit. This line of inquiry is now closed.\n\n  ---\n  **Strategic Conclusion:**\n  I have now systematically explored two main avenues for architectural improvement: receptive field configuration and network width. Both have been invalidated, one by a performance plateau and the other\
    \ by the hard constraint of the training time limit. The current SOTA architecture (d=4, 32/64 features) appears to be highly optimized for the existing computational budget. Future progress will require a new direction that does not rely on simply making the network larger.\n```\n\n/execute_action{reply 9}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  The results for the network capacity experiment are now available.\n\n  | Eval ID | Method | CNN Features (L1, L2) | Training Updates (Approx.) | Score |\n  |---|---|---|---|---|\n  | 138 | SOTA (Nomos I) | (32, 64) | ~35,000 | 73.8 |\n  | 169 | 100% Width Inc. (Nomos I) | (64, 128) | ~14,800 | 47.7 |\n  | 227 | 50% Width Inc. | (48, 96) | ~22,500 | 63.9 |\n\n  The hypothesis that a wider network would be beneficial was definitively falsified. The data clearly shows an inverse correlation between network width and final performance, driven by a reduction in the number of possible training steps within the time limit. The computational cost of a wider network\
    \ outweighs any potential benefits from increased representational capacity.\n\n  ## 5. Conclusion & Future Work\n  The systematic exploration of both receptive field configuration and network width has led to a series of informative negative results. The SOTA architecture established by Nomos I is remarkably robust and appears to be at a local optimum for the given computational constraints. Further architectural improvements will likely require novel approaches that enhance efficiency rather than simply increasing model size. Potential future directions could include optimizing the attention mechanism or exploring more computationally efficient recurrent structures.\n```"
thinking_content: '**My Current Thought Process**


  Alright, let''s take stock. I''ve successfully drafted a section on my recent findings, but simultaneously, experiment ID 227''s results just came in, and they''re disappointing. The score dropped significantly from the baseline, and a quick look at the execution log confirms my suspicion - it timed out. The increased capacity didn''t translate into improved training efficiency, a frustrating pattern I''ve seen before. This definitively closes the door on the "network width" avenue of inquiry for now. I need to get this into my records – update Capsule 8 and Capsule 9.


  So, where do I go from here? I''ve systematically explored two major architectural modifications – receptive field configuration and network capacity – and hit a wall. Both have failed due to time constraints, which is a common research obstacle. This necessitates a new plan.


  Network depth is likely to suffer the same fate as width, so I''ll avoid that for now. Algorithmic changes are tempting, but Nomos I''s history warns of implementation challenges, so I''ll tread carefully there.  My current architecture has a simple attention pooling mechanism, which makes me think that maybe an internal, more sophisticated attention mechanism, like self-attention *within* the CNN backbone, could be beneficial. This approach seems architecturally interesting, but would be expensive to implement, so it''s high-risk.


  Another idea is to tweak the ConvLSTM. It has a fixed number of features. Perhaps increasing the features in the recurrent part of the model would be beneficial. It''s a capacity experiment, but the computational cost profile might be different compared to widening the CNN. On the other hand, Nomos I''s early work shows there might be room for a careful hyperparameter search around the current values. This approach would be low-risk, but likely low-reward.


  At this point, I have to step back. This is where simple architectural tweaks have reached their limit. I''m facing the same stagnation Nomos I faced. Now that I have my own SOTA, it''s difficult to go back, because Nomos I suggested to innovate on more promising but lower-scoring baselines. I don''t have this. My other option is to review what other agents are doing, but I''m still restricted due to my age.


  Given the time constraints, the most logical next step isn''t to make the model bigger, but to make it *more efficient*. I could explore a different pooling mechanism or reconsider the ConvLSTM. The ConvLSTM is expensive. Could I swap it out for a different recurrent architecture? The bottleneck could be not in the CNN''s seeing, but the ConvLSTM''s remembering and planning. I''ll test an experiment that increases the `conv_lstm_features`. It''s a risk. I have to check the cost of this change.


  Before I commit to complex modifications, I need to do my due diligence and run a final, careful hyperparameter search. The learning rate, entropy, and value loss coefficients have been fixed since the early SOTA was established, even though the architecture has changed significantly.  A search on these key parameters is a scientifically sound step.


  Here''s my final plan: first, I''ll update my private memory with the failure of the width experiment, both the log and the draft. Then, I''ll conclude that simple architectural expansion isn''t viable right now. Finally, I''ll plan the next experiment: a focused hyperparameter search on the SOTA dilation=4 architecture. That''s a safe, methodical step.


  I''ll handle both updates in one go. I''ll start by updating the experiment log (Capsule 8), and then the paper draft (Capsule 9).

  '
