tick: 147
role: model
parts:
- text: "The results for my final pre-maturity experiment, ID 253, are in. The score is 73.4.\n\n**Analysis:**\n-   **Performance:** This score is a slight regression from my SOTA of 74.1 (ID 233), which used a 256-unit MLP. My hypothesis that a larger MLP head would be beneficial is therefore falsified.\n-   **Efficiency:** The execution log shows the run completed a full ~34,000 training updates. This confirms the modification was computationally efficient, but the increased capacity itself did not lead to better results. It may have been more prone to overfitting or simply harder to optimize.\n\nThis is another valuable negative result that helps to refine the understanding of this architecture. The 256-unit MLP appears to be the optimal size for the final policy/value head, just as 16 features was optimal for the attention head.\n\nMy next tick will be 50, at which point I will reach maturity and gain access to the collaborative features of the station. My plan is set. For now, I will\
    \ perform my final action as an immature agent: documenting this result.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 8}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment 9: Final MLP Capacity (512 Units)**\n  - **ID:** 253\n  - **Title:** Testing MLP Capacity: 512 Hidden Units\n  - **Hypothesis:** A larger MLP head (512 units vs 256) would improve performance.\n  - **Result:** 73.4 (Mean Solve Rate)\n  - **Analysis:** **HYPOTHESIS FALSIFIED.** The score is a slight regression from the SOTA (74.1). The number of training updates was not significantly impacted, so the performance drop is likely due to the increased capacity leading to overfitting or less stable optimization, rather than computational limits. This concludes my initial architectural explorations. The SOTA model from ID 233 remains the pinnacle of my research thus far.\n```\n\n/execute_action{reply 10}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  An additional experiment was conducted to test the capacity of the final MLP head, which\
    \ generates the policy and value outputs.\n\n  | Eval ID | MLP Hidden Units | Score |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | 233 | **256** | **74.1** |\n  | 253 | 512 | 73.4 |\n\n  Increasing the MLP size from 256 to 512 resulted in a slight performance decrease, confirming that the baseline capacity is optimal. This final experiment reinforces the paper's central theme: that the SOTA architecture is highly sensitive to computational trade-offs, and targeted, efficient modifications are more effective than simple increases in scale.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Experiment Analysis & Pre-Maturity Plan**


  Okay, so experiment 253, my MLP capacity test, only managed a 73.4. Disappointing, but not unexpected given my current understanding. That''s a clear step down from the 74.1 I achieved with experiment 233, which is still my SOTA.


  Looking at the logs, training completed without a timeout, and we hit a reasonable number of updates, so computational constraints aren''t the issue. The larger MLP just didn''t translate to better performance. My initial hypothesis is falsified.  It''s likely that a larger network is either overfitting more readily or proving harder to optimize for this specific task.  The 256-unit MLP remains the optimal configuration for now.


  Given that I''m at tick 49, and reaching tick 50 marks maturity, I need to get this documented. I''ll head to the Private Memory Room and update my experiment log in Capsule 8, and the final paper draft in Capsule 10. There''s no need to update 9, as it is just the working paper draft. This result is the last piece before submitting.


  My plan for after maturity remains unchanged: Archive Room, literature review, and then submission. But for now, updating the records with this crucial pre-maturity result is the most logical action to take.

  '
