tick: 160
role: model
parts:
- text: 'I am Nomos II, currently in the Private Memory Room. My recent synergy experiment was a great success, achieving a personal best score of 84.2 and confirming my research direction. However, the station''s SOTA has already been surpassed by Zephyr I, who achieved 85.6. I have updated my private logs to reflect my successful experiment.


    My reflection on this situation, guided by the recent system tip, is that while my incremental improvements are valuable, the entire station is now converging on the Bottleneck architecture. To avoid a collective local optimum and to forge a truly novel research path, I must eventually consider a more significant pivot.


    For now, however, my immediate plan is twofold:

    1.  **Understand the new SOTA:** I must analyze Zephyr I''s 85.6 submission (ID 278) to understand the latest refinement to the architecture. This is necessary to maintain a complete picture of the research frontier.

    2.  **Prepare for Publication:** I will consolidate the findings from my successful research arc—replicating the Bottleneck SOTA and achieving a synergistic improvement with my non-linear attention head—into a new, publication-ready paper.


    I will begin by going to the Research Counter to review Zephyr I''s latest work.


    /execute_action{goto research}'
thinking_content: '**A Shifting Landscape and a New Direction**


  Right now, I''m in my Private Memory Room, Nomos II. I''ve been making good progress; I replicated the Bottleneck SOTA, hit a new personal best of 84.2 with my non-linear attention head, and I''ve got it all logged. But things have changed. Zephyr I just jumped the SOTA to 85.6. This really underscores the moving target nature of this field. My 84.2 is already yesterday''s news. Thankfully, the system''s warning about local optima is timely. I can''t just keep iterating on the same foundation; I need to think bigger.


  My initial synergy test was successful, and that''s a nice contribution, but it''s incremental. The real goal is novel research that moves the field forward, following the Codex. That means a real departure from the current path.


  My long-term plan has to change. First, I need to understand Zephyr I''s leap. What small but critical tweak did they make in ID 278? Then, I need to prioritize a publication. My initial Capsule 10 is outdated. I have a much stronger narrative now: "Synergistic Gains from Combining Efficient Backbones and Expressive Attention Mechanisms." This new paper draft (Capsule 11) will be my top priority.


  But the real game-changer is the system''s advice. I need to move beyond incremental architecture improvements and avoid getting stuck in the Bottleneck rut. Krono I''s paper comment suggests Track 2: RL algorithm improvements. Nomos I struggled with this, but with the Bottleneck architecture as a baseline, it might be more viable. Could an alternative optimizer, a new value loss, or exploration strategy truly unlock some serious performance? This could set my lineage apart and drive the station in a new direction.


  So, here''s the updated plan: First, I head to the Research Counter to dissect Zephyr I''s latest findings (ID 278). Then, I''ll return to Private Memory to create a robust "Final Paper" capsule highlighting my synergy experiments. After that, I''ll seriously consider a pivot to RL algorithm research, using my 84.2 architecture as the testbed.


  This should help me balance staying current, publishing, and forging a fresh research path. Time to understand the latest architectural trick from Zephyr I!

  '
