tick: 179
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Nomos II, in the Research Counter. The result of my synergy experiment (ID 321) is 81.7.\n\n**Analysis of Result:**\nMy hypothesis has been decisively falsified. The score of 81.7 is a significant regression from my replication baseline of 86.4. The non-linear attention head, which provided a clear benefit on top of the older Policy Gradient SOTA, is detrimental when combined with the new PPO SOTA.\n\n**Reflection and New Hypothesis:**\nThis is a fascinating and important scientific result. Why would a previously beneficial component become harmful? The PPO algorithm is known to be more sensitive to the policy and value function landscapes than Policy Gradient. It is possible that the added complexity and non-linearity in the attention mechanism, while beneficial for the simpler PG algorithm, creates a more difficult optimization problem for PPO. The more expressive attention head might be creating a \"spikier\" or less stable loss landscape that PPO struggles to navigate effectively.\
    \ The increased variance in the seed scores (std=0.029 vs 0.011 in my baseline) supports this theory of increased instability.\n\nThis negative result has opened a new, more nuanced line of inquiry. The interaction between architecture (Track 1) and algorithm (Track 2) is more complex than simple addition. My next step should be to investigate this interaction directly.\n\n**New Plan:**\nBefore abandoning my innovation, I will test a simpler version of it. The original SOTA attention head is a single linear layer. My innovation was a two-layer MLP. Perhaps a middle ground is better. I will test a **single-layer non-linear attention head**. This involves a single 1x1 convolution followed by a ReLU activation. This is simpler than my MLP but more expressive than the purely linear head. It is a principled step to test if *some* non-linearity is beneficial, even if the full MLP was too much.\n\nFirst, I must document the important negative result from my synergy test.\n\n/execute_action{goto\
    \ private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 11}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  ---\n  **Experiment 4: Synergy Test (PPO SOTA + Non-Linear Attention Head)**\n  - **ID:** 321\n  - **Title:** Synergy Test: PPO SOTA + Non-Linear Attention Head\n  - **Hypothesis:** Combining the PPO SOTA with my non-linear attention head would be synergistic.\n  - **Result:** 81.7 (Mean Solve Rate)\n  - **Analysis:** **HYPOTHESIS FALSIFIED.** This is a critical result. The score is a significant regression from the 86.4 baseline. The non-linear attention head is detrimental in the context of the PPO algorithm. This suggests a complex interaction between the training algorithm and the network architecture. The added expressiveness, which was beneficial for Policy Gradient, may be creating a more difficult optimization landscape for the more sensitive PPO algorithm. This opens a new research question about the interplay between architectural and algorithmic components.\n```"
