tick: 191
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Nomos II, back in the Research Counter. My token budget is secure, and I have a clear mandate for my next research phase. My recent experiments have conclusively shown negative synergy between my architectural innovations and the PPO algorithm, as well as between Zephyr I's \"late time injection\" and PPO. The station SOTA (86.7) remains the simple Bottleneck architecture trained with PPO.\n\n**Reflection and Hypothesis Formulation:**\nThe core question is: *what specific architectural features make PPO unstable?*\n-   My non-linear attention head, beneficial with Policy Gradient, was detrimental.\n-   Zephyr's late time injection, beneficial with Policy Gradient, was detrimental.\n-   The common thread in both my architecture and Zephyr's is a more complex final MLP head that processes additional or transformed information (attention-weighted features in my case, a time vector in Zephyr's). The successful PPO SOTA has a very simple MLP head.\n\n**Hypothesis:** The PPO algorithm,\
    \ as implemented, is highly sensitive to the complexity of the policy and value heads. Architectures with more complex MLP heads, even if they provide more information, may create a more difficult optimization landscape for PPO's clipped objective, leading to suboptimal convergence.\n\nTo test this, I can perform a very precise ablation. I will take the successful \"late time injection\" architecture, which uses a deeper MLP head, and replace its head with the simpler, shallower MLP head from the PPO SOTA architecture.\n\n**Plan:**\n1.  **Create a Hybrid Architecture File:** I will create a new Python file in my storage (`nomos/late_injection_simple_head.py`) that contains Zephyr I's architecture but uses the simpler final MLP from Krono II's PPO SOTA.\n2.  **Submit the Experiment with PPO:** I will then train this new hybrid architecture using the PPO training step.\n\nIf this new hybrid model scores higher than my previous attempt (84.5) and closer to the SOTA (86.7), it will provide\
    \ strong evidence that MLP head complexity is indeed a key factor in PPO compatibility.\n\nI will now create the architecture file.\n\n/execute_action{storage write nomos/late_injection_simple_head.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Architecture combining Zephyr's Late Time Injection with the SIMPLER MLP head from Krono's PPO SOTA.\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Optional, Dict\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4 * self.features, (3, 3), padding='SAME', kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i, f, o = nn.sigmoid(i), nn.sigmoid(f), nn.sigmoid(o); g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          new_c = f * c + i * g\n          new_h = o * jnp.tanh(new_c)\n      \
    \    return (new_h, new_c), new_h\n\n  class BottleneckBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      ratio: float = 0.25\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          mid = max(1, int(self.features * self.ratio))\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (1,1), padding='SAME', kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1), padding='SAME', kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class LateInjectionSimpleHead(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int = 32\n      cnn_features_2: int = 64\n      conv_lstm_features: int = 64\n      dilation: int = 5\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.25\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: Optional[jnp.ndarray], rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          x_spat = x[..., :7]\n          t = jnp.mean(x[...,\
    \ 7:8], axis=(1,2))\n          B, H, W, _ = x_spat.shape\n\n          cnn_out = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation), kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x_spat)\n          cnn_out = nn.relu(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_1, ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio)(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation), kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = nn.relu(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_2, ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio)(cnn_out)\n\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B, H, W, self.conv_lstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': zeros(), 'c': zeros()}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h']\
    \ * m, 'c': rnn_state['c'] * m}\n\n          cell = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)\n          (h1, c1), _ = cell((rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']), cnn_out)\n          (h2, c2), h_out = cell((h1, c1), cnn_out)\n          new_rnn_state = {'h': h2, 'c': c2}\n\n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME', kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(h_out)\n          attn = nn.softmax(attn_logits.reshape(B, -1), axis=-1)\n          z_spat = jnp.einsum('bhc,bh->bc', h_out.reshape(B, -1, h_out.shape[-1]), attn)\n\n          z = jnp.concatenate([z_spat, t], axis=-1)\n\n          # HYBRID PART: Use the simpler MLP head from the PPO SOTA\n          z = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z)\n          z = nn.relu(z)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, new_rnn_state\n\
    ```"
