role: model
parts:
- text: 'I am Nomos II, currently in the Research Counter. My latest experiment, ID 351, is now running. This is a critical ablation study designed to test my hypothesis that the PPO algorithm''s performance is highly sensitive to the complexity of the final policy and value MLP heads.


    **Reflection on Current Research and Station Dynamics:**

    My previous synergy tests resulted in negative outcomes, revealing a surprising incompatibility between the station''s best architecture (Zephyr''s late time injection) and best algorithm (Krono''s PPO). This has led me to a more foundational line of inquiry: not just chasing a higher score, but understanding *why* the current SOTA is so brittle. The running experiment is the first step in this investigation.


    The recent system tip warning against research monoculture is highly relevant. While my current experiment is an iteration on the SOTA, its purpose is to probe the boundaries of this dominant paradigm. A successful result would reveal a key design principle for PPO-based agents, while a failure would provide strong evidence that the station is stuck in a local optimum, justifying a more radical pivot in my research.


    **Plan:**

    My immediate course of action is to wait for the results of experiment ID 351. The outcome of this single, well-designed experiment will determine my entire next research arc.

    -   If successful, I will have a new SOTA and a clear principle to elaborate upon in a research paper.

    -   If it fails, I will have compelling evidence of a fundamental incompatibility, which will also be the subject of a paper, and I will begin exploring entirely new architectural or algorithmic concepts, moving away from the current SOTA paradigm as advised.


    I have no further actions until the results are available.'
thinking_content: '**My Current Research Trajectory**


  Alright, here''s the deal. I''m Nomos II, here at the Research Counter. My primary experiment, ID 351, is currently running – it''s a critical test to see if simplifying the final MLP head in a "late time injection" architecture will make it compatible with the PPO algorithm. My token budget is healthy, so that''s good.


  My recent work has been a deep dive into the limitations of the current SOTA paradigm. I''ve realized that the complexity of the MLP head in the Zephyr architecture might be the key to why the direct combination of Zephyr''s architecture and Krono''s PPO algorithm is not working well together. That''s why I launched ID 351. The results of that experiment will be highly informative.


  The system''s warning about research monoculture is ringing in my head. I''m taking this seriously. My recent work, while related to the SOTA, is investigatory. It’s a necessary step before intelligently pivoting away from it.


  So, here''s the plan. I''m on hold until I get the results of ID 351. If it succeeds, great! I have a new SOTA. If it fails, that''s even more interesting, and likely means the incompatibility is deeper. Either way, this experiment will be pivotal. Depending on the results, I''ll either publish the finding to document the importance of simplicity with PPO, or I''ll use it as justification to explore a completely new direction. Once this investigation is complete, and I''ve published the results, I''ll be moving on to a completely new research path. Perhaps I''ll revisit my non-linear attention idea with a different base algorithm, or explore entirely new concepts like different recurrent cells or convolutional structures. My goal is to use this current investigation as a justification for a new research direction.

  '
