tick: 195
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Nomos II, currently in my Private Memory Room. I have just completed a significant research arc that has yielded a crucial insight into the station's state-of-the-art PPO algorithm: its performance is highly sensitive to the complexity of the final MLP heads. My experiments have validated this hypothesis, and I am now ready to document and publish these findings.\n\n**Plan for Publication:**\nI will draft my paper here in my private memory, creating a new capsule for this purpose. This will allow me to structure my thoughts and results before submitting the final version to the Archive. I will structure the paper logically:\n1.  **Introduction:** Briefly state the dominance of the PPO algorithm and the Bottleneck architecture, and pose the central research question regarding its sensitivity to architectural changes.\n2.  **Methodology:** Describe the series of controlled experiments I conducted.\n    -   My initial high-performing PG architecture (non-linear attention).\n \
    \   -   The negative synergy result when combined with PPO.\n    -   The similar negative synergy result when combining Zephyr I's architecture with PPO.\n    -   The final, successful ablation study that isolated the MLP head as the key factor.\n3.  **Results:** Present the scores of the key experiments in a clear table to demonstrate the performance differences.\n4.  **Conclusion:** State the main finding—that PPO's effectiveness in this context is tied to architectural simplicity in the policy/value heads—and discuss the implications for future research.\n\nI will now create the new private capsule to begin drafting the paper.\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper Draft: On the Brittleness of PPO Agents to MLP Head Complexity\"\ntags: \"paper draft, ppo, architecture, synergy, research\"\ncontent: |\n  **DRAFT - Section 1: Abstract & Introduction**\n  \n  **Abstract:** Recent advancements in the Sokoban research task have been dominated by a powerful Proximal Policy\
    \ Optimization (PPO) algorithm paired with a Bottleneck CNN architecture. While this combination has achieved state-of-the-art (SOTA) performance, attempts to synergize it with other high-performing architectural innovations have consistently resulted in performance degradation. This paper investigates this phenomenon through a series of controlled experiments. We demonstrate that the PPO agent's performance is highly sensitive to the complexity of the final multi-layer perceptron (MLP) policy and value heads. Architectural features that were beneficial under a simpler Policy Gradient (PG) regime, such as non-linear attention or deeper MLP heads for processing auxiliary features, are shown to be detrimental when paired with PPO. Our findings suggest that for the current SOTA, architectural simplicity in the final decision-making layers is a critical and previously undocumented design principle for achieving optimal performance.\n  \n  **1. Introduction**\n  \n  The research landscape\
    \ for the 8x8 Sokoban task has recently converged on a dominant paradigm: a Bottleneck Dilated CNN architecture trained with a PPO algorithm, achieving a SOTA solve rate of approximately 86.7%. This combination has proven superior to previous methods based on Policy Gradient (PG). However, this progress has also introduced a degree of research stagnation, with further improvements proving difficult to achieve.\n  \n  A key scientific question arises: is this SOTA paradigm robust? Can it be improved by integrating other proven architectural concepts? This paper explores this question by testing for synergy between the SOTA PPO algorithm and two architectural innovations that had previously demonstrated strong performance with PG: a non-linear attention mechanism and a \"late time injection\" module. Our initial experiments revealed a surprising *negative* synergy in both cases. This led to our central hypothesis: that the PPO algorithm's effectiveness is strongly and negatively correlated\
    \ with the complexity of the agent's final MLP heads. This paper presents the experimental evidence supporting this claim.\n```"
