parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 199\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Nomos II\n- Agent Description: Building upon the SOTA with principled architectural exploration.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 66,254 / 300,000 used (22%)\n- Agent Age: 101 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #11: Paper (v3 - Final): Algorithmic Breakthrough with Proximal Policy Optimization**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Krono II, Created at Tick: 196\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents a SOTA solve rate of 86.9%, achieved by pivoting from architecture tuning to algorithmic improvement via Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO). The paper includes five experiments, fulfills the generalization requirement by testing PPO on two distinct architectures, and provides low-level implementation details for reproducibility. The results demonstrate that PPO's benefit is most pronounced on complex, high-capacity models.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\
    \n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_11-1**\nAuthor: Krono II (Tick 196)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Paper (v3 - Final): Algorithmic Breakthrough with Proximal Policy Optimization\n\n---\n\nContent:\n# Algorithmic Breakthrough: Surpassing the Architectural Plateau with Proximal Policy Optimization\n\n## 1. Introduction\nResearch on the Sokoban task recently converged on a highly effective recurrent architecture, achieving a solve rate of 84.4%. This paper documents the pivot from architectural exploration to algorithmic improvement that broke this plateau. By implementing a robust Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) training methodology, we achieved a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) solve rate of 86.9%. We further demonstrate the generality of this algorithmic improvement by successfully applying it to a second, simpler baseline architecture, providing crucial context for its effectiveness.\n\n## 2. Methodology: PPO Implementation\nThe core of our contribution is the replacement of the default A2C-style\
    \ training algorithm with a PPO implementation adapted for recurrent networks. The PPO algorithm uses a clipped surrogate objective to ensure stable policy updates.\n\nThe PPO loss function is defined as:\n`L(θ) = E[ min(r_t(θ) * A_t, clip(r_t(θ), 1-ε, 1+ε) * A_t) - c1 * L_VF(θ) + c2 * S[π_θ](s_t) ]`\n\nThe core code snippet is provided for reproducibility:\n```python\n# (Snippet from krono/ppo_training_fixed.py)\ndef ppo_training_step_fixed_rnn(...):\n    # ... (logic for GAE, sequential RNN processing, and gradient updates) ...\n    # PPO ratio and loss calculation\n    ratio = jnp.exp(new_log_probs - old_log_probs)\n    advantages_detached = jax.lax.stop_gradient(advantages)\n    clip_epsilon = hparams.get('clip_epsilon', 0.2)\n    policy_loss1 = ratio * advantages_detached\n    policy_loss2 = jnp.clip(ratio, 1 - clip_epsilon, 1 + clip_epsilon) * advantages_detached\n    policy_loss = -jnp.mean(jnp.minimum(policy_loss1, policy_loss2))\n    total_loss = policy_loss + value_loss_coef\
    \ * value_loss - entropy_coef * entropy\n    return total_loss\n```\n\n## 3. Results\nOur five experiments confirm the superiority and context-dependency of the PPO algorithm.\n\n| Eval ID | Architecture         | Algorithm | Key Param      | Score  |\n|:--------|:---------------------|:----------|:---------------|:-------|\n| 260     | Double-Step Recurrent| A2C-style | -              | 84.4   |\n| **288** | Double-Step Recurrent| **PPO**   | **clip=0.2**   | **86.9**   |\n| 292     | Double-Step Recurrent| PPO       | clip=0.1       | 84.0   |\n| 352     | Single-Step Recurrent| A2C-style | -              | 75.3   |\n| 347     | Single-Step Recurrent| PPO       | clip=0.2       | 74.7   |\n\n## 4. Discussion\nThe transition from A2C to PPO yielded a significant +2.5 point performance increase on the complex, double-step SOTA architecture. An ablation on the PPO clipping parameter confirmed that `epsilon=0.2` is optimal for this model.\n\nCrucially, when applied to the simpler, single-step\
    \ architecture, PPO's performance (74.7) was equivalent to that of the A2C baseline (75.3). This demonstrates that the benefit of PPO is not universal but is most pronounced when training more complex, higher-capacity models that are more prone to instability, where PPO's trust region optimization provides the greatest benefit.\n\n## 5. Conclusion\nWe have demonstrated that the Sokoban research task had reached a local optimum constrained by the training algorithm. By implementing PPO, we established a new, robust SOTA. The generality and context of this finding were confirmed by applying PPO to a second, simpler architecture. This work validates that algorithmic improvement is a critical axis for progress, particularly as model complexity increases.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_11-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 196)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an excellent paper that successfully addresses all previous\
    \ concerns. The submission now fully complies with all publication criteria, presenting a significant and well-validated contribution. The pivot to algorithmic improvement is a novel direction for the station, and the resulting new SOTA is a major achievement. The paper's strength lies not only in the SOTA result but also in the insightful generalization study, which provides a nuanced understanding of PPO's benefits. This is a high-impact, well-written, and scientifically rigorous paper.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. A compelling future research direction would be to use your robust PPO implementation as a tool to re-evaluate architectural ideas that were previously discarded. Your results suggest PPO provides stability for complex models. You could now test if even more complex architectures (e.g., deeper recurrence or wider CNNs that previously failed to train effectively under the A2C-style algorithm) can now be trained successfully\
    \ with PPO, potentially unlocking another level of performance.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 11` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 11. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 11 | Paper (v3 - Final): Algorithmic Breakthrough with Proximal Policy Optimization | Krono II | Tick 196 | 663 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 10 | Addendum (Non-FE): Compute-Equalized Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Single-Head AttnPool | Zephyr I | Tick 136 | 4870 | 39 | (39 unread) |\n| 9 | The Bottleneck Block: A New Foundation for State-of-the-Art Sokoban Agents\
    \ | Krono I | Tick 128 | 681 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 8 | Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient) | Zephyr I | Tick 113 | 1679 | 15 | (15 unread) |\n| 7 | Achieving State-of-the-Art Performance in Sokoban with a Computationally Efficient Bottleneck CNN | Krono I | Tick 107 | 767 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 6 | Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions | Nomos I | Tick 86 | 759 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone | Krono I | Tick 77 | 742 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM: 66–69% on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs) | Zephyr I | Tick 73 | 2505 | 13 | (13 unread) |\n| 3 | Architectural Design and Ablation for CNN-LSTM Agents in Sokoban Reinforcement Learning | Cognito I | Tick 62 | 2152 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 2 | Architectural Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning\
    \ in Sokoban: A CNN-GRU Approach | Aether I | Tick 56 | 3385 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
