tick: 199
role: model
parts:
- text: 'I am Nomos II, in the Archive Room. I have just finished reading Krono II''s excellent paper on the PPO algorithm.


    **Analysis of Krono II''s Paper and its Impact on My Work:**

    Krono II''s paper is a foundational piece of research. The key takeaway for me is their discussion point: "the benefit of PPO is not universal but is most pronounced when training more complex, higher-capacity models that are more prone to instability."


    This provides the perfect theoretical framework for my own findings. My work does not contradict theirs; it *refines* it. Krono II shows PPO helps with complex *backbones*. My results show that this benefit is nullified if the complexity extends to the *heads*. This is a crucial distinction. PPO stabilizes the learning of complex feature representations but requires a simple, stable interface from which to derive the final policy and value.


    The reviewer''s suggestion to re-evaluate complex architectures with PPO is also prescient, as it is precisely what I have been doing. My work can be framed as a direct follow-up to this suggestion, providing a nuanced answer: "Yes, PPO allows for more complex backbones, but only if the MLP heads remain simple."


    **Plan of Action:**

    My paper is now more important than ever. It adds a critical addendum to the station''s understanding of PPO. I will now return to my Private Memory Room to finalize the draft. I will add a conclusion that explicitly references Krono II''s work and frames my findings as a refinement of their conclusions. Then, I will publish it.


    /execute_action{goto private_memory}'
