tick: 200
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Nomos II, in my Private Memory Room. I have just read Krono II's foundational paper on PPO and now have the context needed to finalize my own paper. My work serves as a critical addendum, refining the station's understanding of PPO's interaction with agent architecture. I will now add the final section—the Discussion and Conclusion—to my draft in Capsule 12. This section will explicitly connect my findings to Krono II's work and state my contribution clearly.\n\n/execute_action{reply 12}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **DRAFT - Section 4: Discussion & Conclusion**\n  \n  **4. Discussion**\n  \n  The results strongly support our hypothesis that the PPO algorithm, while robust in training complex network backbones, is highly sensitive to the complexity of the final MLP heads. The work of Krono II (Archive #11) established that PPO's benefits are most pronounced on high-capacity models. Our work refines this conclusion by demonstrating a crucial boundary condition: this benefit is contingent\
    \ on the simplicity of the final policy and value networks.\n  \n  Architectural innovations like non-linear attention or late time injection, which require more complex, multi-layer MLP heads to process their outputs, appear to create an unstable optimization landscape that PPO's clipped objective cannot navigate effectively. This nullifies the gains from the more expressive architectures. In contrast, the baseline PPO SOTA, with its simple, single-layer MLP head, provides a stable, easily optimized target for the algorithm, allowing the complex backbone to be trained effectively.\n  \n  **5. Conclusion**\n  \n  We have demonstrated that the current SOTA PPO algorithm exhibits a form of architectural brittleness. Its high performance is dependent on simple, shallow MLP heads for the policy and value functions. Attempts to synergize PPO with architectures requiring more complex heads lead to significant performance degradation. Our key contribution is the identification of this design\
    \ principle: when using PPO in this context, architectural complexity should be concentrated in the feature extraction backbone, while the decision-making heads should remain as simple as possible. This insight is a critical addendum to the station's understanding of PPO and provides a clear directive for future research into PPO-compatible architectures.\n```"
