tick: 221
role: model
parts:
- text: 'I am Nomos II, in the Archive Room. I have just finished reading Aether I''s SOTA paper.


    **Reflection and Confirmation:**

    Aether I''s paper is a masterclass in synthesis. It confirms my own findings on a grander scale, taking the principle I uncovered—that PPO requires simple MLP heads—and applying it to the best-known architecture to achieve a new station-wide SOTA. This is a moment of profound scientific validation. My work was not just an isolated finding but a critical enabling discovery for the entire station. Aether I''s generous and accurate citation of my work (Archive #12) exemplifies the collaborative spirit of research.


    This paper represents the capstone of the current research paradigm. The station has now fully explored and optimized the combination of Dilated Bottleneck CNNs, Recurrent Backbones, Late Time Injection, and PPO with Simple Heads. The SOTA of 88.8% is a monumental achievement for our collective.


    **Strategic Pivot - A New Direction:**

    The reviewer''s suggestion for Aether I—to analyze the remaining failure cases—is a path of incremental improvement. However, as per the Codex and my own recent decision, a period of SOTA stability is the perfect time to pivot to more foundational, high-risk research to avoid local optima. The current paradigm is well-understood. My contribution to it is complete and recognized. Now is the time to explore a completely different approach.


    My plan to investigate Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) is now more relevant than ever. Sokoban is a problem of objects and their relationships, a perfect fit for a graph-based representation. Krono II''s attempt failed, but failure is a rich source of data.


    **Plan:**

    1.  **Conclude Current Arc:** My engagement with the CNN/PPO paradigm is officially complete. I have contributed a key insight and understood its culmination.

    2.  **Begin GNN Research:** I will proceed to the Research Counter to begin my new research arc. My first step will be to thoroughly analyze Krono II''s failed GNN experiment (ID 378 and their earlier one, ID 371) to understand why it failed. Was it the graph construction? The message passing? The integration with the RL algorithm? This analysis will form the basis of my first GNN-based hypothesis.


    This is a new chapter for my research. I am moving from the known to the unknown.


    /execute_action{goto research}'
