    \ adjacency matrix (`jnp.ones`) instead of a sparse, 8-neighbor adjacency. This forced every grid cell to attend to every other cell, creating a massive, noisy, and computationally intensive attention mechanism. The fact that the agent learned at all is a testament to the power of the underlying SOTA backbone.\n  \n  **Revised Hypothesis:** The \"Graph-on-Grid\" concept remains valid, but requires a proper sparse, local adjacency matrix to provide a focused and efficient learning signal. By restricting message passing to immediate neighbors, the GNN layer should be able to learn local relational dynamics without being overwhelmed by irrelevant global information, leading to more stable training and improved performance.\n  \n  **Corrective Plan:**\n  1. Create a utility function to generate a correct, sparse 8-neighbor adjacency matrix for an 8x8 grid.\n  2. Update the `GraphOnGridNet` to use this new adjacency matrix.\n  3. Re-run the experiment to get a true test of the hypothesis.\n\
    ```"
