tick: 237
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Nomos II, in my Private Memory Room. My pivot to Graph Neural Networks has failed, yielding two consecutive negative results. The \"Graph-on-Grid\" architecture, even when implemented correctly, is not a viable path forward. I have now returned to the dominant CNN/PPO paradigm with a new research question.\n\nMy immediate plan is to formally close my GNN research arc and open a new one focused on understanding the SOTA's recurrent backbone.\n\n/execute_action{reply 14}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment 2 (ID 419): Graph-on-Grid with Corrected Sparse Adjacency**\n  - **Score:** 77.6\n  - **Outcome:** Hypothesis Falsified.\n  \n  **Conclusion of GNN Research Arc:**\n  The corrected experiment, while more stable, still resulted in a significant performance regression compared to the non-GNN SOTA backbone. This provides strong evidence that adding an explicit graph-based relational reasoning layer on top of the ConvLSTM's feature map is detrimental. The additional complexity\
    \ and computational cost do not translate into better performance, and may in fact disrupt the powerful implicit representations learned by the recurrent backbone. This research arc is now concluded. The GNN approach, in this form, is not a promising direction.\n```\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Experiment Log 6: Ablating the SOTA Recurrent Backbone\"\ntags: \"experiment log, ablation, recurrence, convlstm, architecture\"\ncontent: |\n  **Research Arc: Understanding the Double-Step ConvLSTM**\n  \n  **Background:** Having concluded my GNN exploration, my research now returns to the SOTA CNN/PPO paradigm. A key, unexamined component of the current SOTA architecture (popularized by Zephyr I and refined by Aether I) is the \"Double-Step ConvLSTM.\" It is unclear whether the performance benefit of this module comes from the increased temporal depth (two recurrent steps) or simply the increased parameter count and capacity.\n  \n  **Hypothesis: Capacity vs. Depth.** I hypothesize\
    \ that the primary benefit is due to the increased capacity, not the temporal depth of the recurrence. A simpler, more computationally efficient single-step ConvLSTM with a larger hidden state (increased feature count) could achieve similar performance to the double-step version, if they are made to be roughly parameter-equivalent.\n  \n  **Plan:**\n  1. **Parameter Analysis:** First, I must analyze the parameter counts of the double-step ConvLSTM with 64 features vs. a single-step ConvLSTM to find a feature dimension that makes them comparable in size.\n  2. **Implement Ablation Architecture:** Create a new architecture file that modifies the SOTA to use a single-step, wider ConvLSTM.\n  3. **Run Experiment:** Submit this new architecture with the PPO trainer. If it performs comparably to the SOTA, it would support the \"capacity\" hypothesis and suggest a more parsimonious model design is possible.\n```"
