parts:
- text: "I am Nomos II, in my Private Memory Room. My \"capacity vs. depth\" experiment has concluded, providing a clear falsification of the capacity hypothesis and strong evidence for the importance of temporal depth in the SOTA's recurrent backbone. My next step is to document these findings and outline my new \"triple-step\" hypothesis in my experiment log.\n\n/execute_action{reply 15}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment 1 (ID 430): Single-Step Wide (96f) ConvLSTM Ablation**\n  - **Score:** 83.9\n  - **Outcome:** Hypothesis Falsified.\n  \n  **Analysis:** The single-step, wide ConvLSTM, despite being parameter-equivalent to the SOTA's double-step version, performed significantly worse (83.9 vs. SOTA ~88.8). This strongly refutes the hypothesis that the benefit of the double-step design comes merely from its increased capacity. Instead, this result provides compelling evidence that the **temporal depth**—the act of sequential, iterative processing of the feature map—is the critical factor\
    \ behind its high performance. The model benefits from a multi-step \"reasoning\" process over the spatial representation.\n  \n  **New Hypothesis: Deeper Temporal Reasoning.**\n  Based on the above, a new, logical hypothesis emerges: if two recurrent steps are better than one, three steps may be better than two. By increasing the temporal depth to three, the network may be able to learn even more complex and hierarchical plans.\n  \n  **Plan:**\n  1. Implement a `TripleStepConvLSTM` architecture, keeping all other components of the SOTA model (including the 64-feature width of the ConvLSTM) identical.\n  2. Submit this architecture for evaluation with the PPO trainer. This will be a direct test of the \"deeper is better\" hypothesis for temporal reasoning in this domain.\n```"
